CHAPTER I GENERAL PROVISIONS

Article 1 (Purpose) The purpose of this Act is to enhance the technical competitiveness of industries and to contribute to the development of the national economy by encouraging invention and facilitating the prompt and efficient securing of rights to inventions and the commercialization thereof.

Article 2 (Definitions) The terms used in this Act are defined as follows: <Amended on Jan. 27, 2010; Jun. 8, 2010; Jul. 30, 2013; May 18, 2015; Jan. 3, 2023; Feb. 6, 2024>
  1. The term "invention" means an invention, design, or creation eligible for protection under the Patent Act, the Utility Model Act, or the Design Protection Act;
  2. The term "service invention" means an invention that an employee, executive officer of a corporation, or public official (hereinafter referred to as "employee, etc.") makes in connection with his or her duties, where it falls within the scope of business of the employer, the corporation, the State, or the competent local government (hereinafter referred to as "employer, etc.") and the activities that have led to the invention fall within the present or past duties of the employee, etc.;
  3. The term "independent inventor" means a person who makes an invention, other than a service invention;
  4. The term "industrial property rights" means rights to a patent, utility model, design, or trademark registered pursuant to the Patent Act, the Utility Model Act, the Design Protection Act, or the Trademark Act;
  5. The term "patent management department" means a department of the employer, etc. in charge of planning, research, management, etc. of industrial property rights;
  5-2. The term "public patent attorney" means a patent attorney who executes his or her duties at a patent consulting center of public patent lawyers established under Article 26-2;
  6. Deleted; <Feb. 6, 2024>
  7. Deleted; <Feb. 6, 2024>
  8. Deleted; <Feb. 6, 2024>
  9. The term "industrial property rights service industry" means any of the following business that support the creation, protection, and utilization of industrial property rights:
    (a) The business of collecting, analyzing, processing, translating, distributing, or managing information on industrial property rights, or of developing or establishing relevant software or systems;
    (b) The business under Article 2 of the Patent Attorney Act;
    (c) The business of evaluating the economic value or technical excellence of industrial property rights, to be expressed in equivalent values, grades, scores, etc.;
    (d) The business of mediating or assisting transactions of industrial property rights, such as transfer of industrial property rights or grant and permission of licenses;
    (e) Any other business prescribed by Presidential Decree;
  10. The term "industrial property rights service provider" means a person providing industrial property rights services;
  11. The term "evaluation of an invention, etc." means expressing the present or future economic value of any of the following in equivalent values, grades, scores, etc.:
    (a) An invention for which an application is pending in the Republic of Korea or a foreign country or which is registered, and a trademark under Article 2 (1) 1 of the Trademark Act (hereinafter referred to as "trademark");
    (b) A trade secret defined in subparagraph 2 of Article 2 of the Unfair Competition Prevention and Trade Secret Protection Act (hereinafter referred to as "trade secret");
    (c) A layout-design defined in subparagraph 2 of Article 2 of the Act on the Layout-Designs of Semiconductor Integrated Circuits (hereinafter referred to as "layout-design").

Article 3 (Comprehensive policy on promotion of invention) (1) The Government shall establish and implement a comprehensive policy on the promotion of invention (hereinafter referred to as "comprehensive invention promotion policy") each year.
  (2) The comprehensive invention promotion policy under paragraph (1) shall include the following matters:
  1. Raising citizens’ awareness of inventions;
  2. Encouraging inventive activities and facilitating securing of rights to outcomes of inventions;
  3. Referral of transfer of outstanding inventions and facilitating commercialization of such inventions;
  4. Other matters necessary for the promotion of inventions.

Article 4 (Grants of subsidies for promotion of invention) (1) The Government may grant a subsidy to any of the following persons within the budget in order to promote inventions: <Amended on Jul. 30, 2013>
  1. An inventor and his or her successor;
  2. An individual or organization that performs research on invention or a project for the promotion of inventions.
  (2) Matters necessary for business activities eligible for subsidies under paragraph (1), the application for, and management of, such subsidies shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Jul. 30, 2013>
  [Title Amended on Jul. 30, 2013]

Article 5 (Invention Day) The Government shall designate May 19 each year as Invention Day in order to make citizens aware of the importance of inventions and stimulate their enthusiasm for inventions and shall hold ceremonies for the promotion of inventions.

CHAPTER II PROMOTION OF INVENTION

SECTION 1 Raising Awareness of Invention

Article 6 (Raising awareness of invention and facilitating inventive activities) The Minister of Intellectual Property shall conduct the following activities in order to raise public awareness of inventions and facilitate inventive activities: <Amended on Jun. 8, 2010; Feb. 6, 2024; Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Holding events for encouraging invention;
  2. Facilitating inventive activities of students, women, and the socially disadvantaged;
  3. Holding exhibitions for outstanding inventions and providing support to outstanding inventors to participate in overseas exhibitions;
  4. Providing information related to inventive activities, etc.;
  5. Providing education and training on invention and industrial property rights;
  6. Identifying and awarding persons of distinguished service to inventions and outstanding inventions;
  7. Other projects necessary for raising public awareness of inventions and facilitating inventive activities.

Article 7 Deleted. <Mar. 14, 2017>

Article 8 (Facilitation of women's inventive activities) (1) The Government shall establish and implement supportive policies for the development of women's inventive creativity and the training of women as outstanding human resources for invention.
  (2) The policy measures under paragraph (1) shall include the following:
  1. Education of women inventors on industrial property rights;
  2. Commercialization of women's inventions;
  3. Matters necessary for promoting women's inventions, such as holding events for promoting women's inventions.

Article 8-2 (Facilitating inventive activities of the socially disadvantaged) (1) The Government shall establish and implement supportive policies to facilitate inventive activities of the socially disadvantaged.
  (2) Supportive policies under paragraph (1) shall include the following matters: <Amended on Jul. 30, 2013; Feb. 6, 2024>
  1. Providing information related to inventive activities, etc. to the socially disadvantaged;
  2. Supporting patent legal services for facilitating inventions by the socially disadvantaged;
  3. Protecting industrial property rights of the socially disadvantaged.
  [This Article Added on Jun. 8, 2010]

Article 9 Deleted. <Mar. 14, 2017>

Article 9-2 Deleted. <Jan. 3, 2023>

SECTION 2 Boosting of Service Invention

Article 10 (Service invention) (1) If an employee, etc. acquires a patent or completes registration of a utility model or design (hereinafter referred to as "patent, etc."), or succeeds to the right to acquire a patent, etc., for a service invention, the employer, etc. shall have a non-exclusive license in the right to a patent, utility model, or design (hereinafter referred to as "patent right, etc."); provided, this shall not apply to an employer, etc. which is an enterprise other than a small and medium enterprise under Article 2 of the Framework Act on Small and Medium Enterprises unless it concludes or prepares, in advance, any of the following contracts or employment regulations subject to consultation with an employee, etc.: <Amended on Jul. 30, 2013>
  1. A contract or employment regulations that makes the employer, etc. succeed to the right to acquire a patent, etc. or a patent right, etc. for a service invention made by the employee, etc.;
  2. A contract or employment regulations that requires the grant of an exclusive license to the employer, etc. for a service invention made by the employee, etc.
  (2) Notwithstanding paragraph (1), the State or a local government may succeed to the rights to a service invention created by a public official or a non-public official belonging to the State or a local government (hereinafter referred to as "public official, etc.") and patent right, etc. of a public official, etc. to such service invention, to which the State or a local government succeeds, shall be State property or public property; provided, an exclusively responsible organization under the latter part of Article 11 (1) of the Technology Transfer and Commercialization Promotion Act (hereinafter referred to as "exclusively responsible organization") may succeed to the rights of a teaching staff member, who works at a national or public school under Article 3 of the Higher Education Act (hereinafter referred to as "national or public school"), to a service invention, and thus the patent right, etc. of a teaching staff member of a national or public school to a service invention, to which the exclusively responsible organization succeeds, shall be owned by the exclusively responsible organization. <Amended on Apr. 20, 2021>
  (3) Any provision of a contract or employment regulations shall be void, if the provision makes an employer, etc. succeed to the right to acquire a patent, etc., or a patent right, etc., in advance, for an invention made by an employee, etc. other than a service invention or requires an employee, etc. to grant an exclusive license in the patent right, etc. to the employer, etc.
  (4) Notwithstanding Article 8 of the State Property Act, the disposal and management of patent rights, etc. (including waiver of patent rights, etc.) that become State property pursuant to paragraph (2) shall be under the jurisdiction of the Minister of Intellectual Property, and matters necessary for the disposal and management thereof shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Jan. 30, 2009; Jan. 27, 2010; Oct. 1, 2025>

Article 10-2 (Special cases concerning dispositions for service invention of public officials, etc.) Notwithstanding the proviso of Article 65-11 (2) of the State Property Act, if determined and publicly notified by the Minister of Intellectual Property, the grant of an exclusive license concerning patent rights, etc. nationalized under Article 10 (2) may be renewed at least once. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  [This Article Added on Apr. 20, 2021]

Article 11 (Implementation of service invention compensation system and supportive policies) (1) The Government shall establish and enforce supportive policies for the implementation of a service invention compensation system, etc. to encourage service inventions.
  (2) Supportive policies under paragraph (1) shall include the following matters: <Amended on Jul. 30, 2013>
  1. Preparation and dissemination of compensation regulations that would serve as standards;
  2. Preparation and dissemination of reasonable procedural regulations for the prevention and resolution of disputes related to compensation;
  3. Support for consultation, etc. on the implementation and operation of the service invention compensation system.
  (3) The Government shall take measures to facilitate the securing of rights to inventions and the commercialization of inventions under Chapters III and IV preferentially for employers, etc. who compensate for service inventions.

Article 11-2 (Certification of outstanding companies in service invention compensation) (1) The Minister of Intellectual Property may certify a company that has set an example in operating the service invention compensation system as an outstanding company in service invention compensation (hereinafter referred to as "outstanding company") in order to promote the service invention compensation system under Article 11 (1). <Amended on Feb. 6, 2024; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) A company that wishes to be certified as an outstanding company shall file an application with the Minister of Intellectual Property. <Added on Feb. 6, 2024; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (3) Upon receipt of an application for certification under paragraph (2), the Minister of Intellectual Property shall examine the company seeking certification and grant a certificate with a validity period if it meets the standards for certification. <Added on Feb. 6, 2024; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (4) The Minister of Intellectual Property may revoke the certification of an outstanding company if it falls under any of the following cases; provided, in cases falling under subparagraph 1, the certification shall be revoked: <Added on Feb. 6, 2024; Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Where it is certified by fraud or other improper means;
  2. Where it no longer meets the standards for certification under paragraph (6).
  (5) The State and local governments may provide administrative and financial support to certified outstanding companies as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Added on Feb. 6, 2024>
  (6) The standards and procedures for certifying outstanding companies, re-certification, and other matters necessary for certification shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Feb. 6, 2024>
  [This Article Added on Mar. 22, 2013]
  [Title Amended on Feb. 6, 2024]

Article 12 (Notice of completion of service invention) An employee, etc. who completes a service invention shall notify the employer, etc. of his or her completion in writing (including an electronic document defined in subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Framework Act on Electronic Documents and Transactions; hereinafter the same shall apply) without delay. Where a service invention is completed jointly by two or more employees, etc., such notice shall be given jointly by the employees, etc. <Amended on Nov. 15, 2022>

Article 13 (Succession of rights to service invention) (1) Where an employer, etc. notified under Article 12 has already concluded or established a contract or employment regulations, based on which the rights to acquire a patent, etc. for an invention of an employee, etc. or patent rights, etc. thereto are transferred or an exclusive license therefor is established, such rights shall be succeeded by the employer, etc.; provided, this shall not apply where the employer, etc. notifies the employee, etc. of its intent not to succeed to the rights to the invention. <Amended on Feb. 6, 2024>
  (2) Where an employer, etc. (excluding the State or a local government) without a contract or employment regulations under paragraph (1) receives a notice under Article 12, the employer, etc. shall notify the employee, etc. in writing as to succession to rights to the invention within the period prescribed by Presidential Decree. In such cases, the employer, etc. may not assert succession to rights to the invention against the intent of the employee, etc. <Amended on Feb. 6, 2024>
  (3) If an employer, etc. does not give notice of whether to succeed to rights to an invention within the period under paragraph (2), the employer, etc. shall be deemed to have waived the succession to the rights to the invention. In such cases, the employer, etc. shall not be entitled to a non-exclusive license without the consent of the employee, etc. who made the invention, notwithstanding Article 10 (1). <Amended on Feb. 6, 2024>
  [Title Amended on Feb. 6, 2024]

Article 14 (Succession to rights to joint invention) Where a service invention has been made jointly by an employee, etc. and a third party and the employer, etc. succeeds to rights to the invention in accordance with a contract or employment regulations, the employer, etc. shall acquire the share of the rights of the employee, etc. to the invention.

Article 15 (Compensation for service invention) (1) An employee, etc. shall be entitled to fair compensation where the employer, etc. succeeds to the right to acquire a patent, etc., or a patent right, etc., for a service invention, or is to be granted an exclusive license in the patent right, etc. under a contract or employment regulations.
  (2) The employer, etc. shall prepare compensation regulations stipulating the criteria for determining the type and amount of compensation referred to in paragraph (1), the method of payment thereof, etc., and shall inform the employee, etc. thereof in writing. <Amended on Jul. 30, 2013; Nov. 15, 2022>
  (3) The employer, etc. shall consult with the employee, etc. regarding the preparation or modification of the compensation regulations under paragraph (2); provided, in the case of a modification disadvantageous to the employee, etc., the employer, etc. shall obtain consent of a majority of the employees, etc. to whom the relevant contract or compensation regulations apply. <Amended on Jul. 30, 2013>
  (4) The employer, etc. shall give a written notice to an employee, etc. who will receive compensation under paragraph (1) of the details of such compensation, including the amount of the compensation determined under the compensation regulations under paragraph (2). <Added on Jul. 30, 2013; Nov. 15, 2022>
  (5) Necessary matters, such as the scope of employees, etc. and procedures, which require consultation or consent under paragraph (3) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Added on Jul. 30, 2013>
  (6) Compensation paid by the employer, etc. to the employee, etc. pursuant to paragraphs (2) through (4) shall be deemed fair compensation; provided, this shall not apply to cases where the amount of compensation fails to factor in the benefits the employer, etc. is anticipated to obtain from a service invention and the degree of contribution by the employer, etc. and employee, etc. to the completion of the invention. <Added on Jul. 30, 2013>
  (7) The State or a local government shall pay fair compensation, when it succeeds to rights to a service invention of a public official, etc. pursuant to Article 10 (2). In such cases, matters necessary for the payment of compensation shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree or municipal ordinance. <Amended on Jul. 30, 2013; Apr. 20, 2021>

Article 16 (Compensation in cases of reserved application filing) An employer, etc. shall pay fair compensation pursuant to Article 15, even in cases where the employer, etc. has succeeded to rights to a service invention, but does not file a patent application or abandons or withdraws a patent application. In such cases, economic benefits that the employee, etc. could have obtained if the invention had been protected by industrial property rights shall be considered in determining the amount of compensation for the invention.

Article 16-2 (Waiver of rights succeeded to and acquisition by employee, etc.) (1) Where a public research institute under subparagraph 6 of Article 2 of the Technology Transfer and Commercialization Promotion Act (hereafter in this Article referred to as "public research institute") succeeds to rights to obtain a patent, etc., or a patent right, etc. for a service invention (hereinafter referred to as "right to a service invention") in the Republic of Korea or overseas and then waivers such right, all the employees, etc. who have contributed to the completion of the relevant service invention may acquire the right to the invention.
  (2) Notwithstanding paragraph (1), where the head of a public research institute deems it particularly necessary to waive a right to a service invention for public interests, he or she need not transfer the right to the employee, etc. In such cases, the head of a public research institute shall notify the employee, etc. of the specific reasons therefor within the period referred to in paragraph (3).
  (3) The head of a public research institute who intends to waiver the right to a service invention pursuant to paragraph (1) shall notify all the employees, etc. involved in the completion of the relevant service invention of such fact within the period prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (4) Upon receipt of a notice under paragraph (3), an employee, etc. who intends to acquire a right to a service invention shall give a written notice of his or her intent to acquire such right to the head of the public research institute within the period prescribed by Presidential Decree from the date of receipt of such notice. <Amended on Nov. 15, 2022>
  (5) Where an employee, etc. gives notice of his or her intent to acquire the right to a service invention pursuant to paragraph (4), such right shall be deemed transferred to the employee, etc. from the day immediately following the expiration of the period referred to in paragraph (4). In such cases, where a public research institute shares the right to a service invention with a third party, it shall be deemed that the right is transferred only when the head of the public research institute obtains consent from all of the other joint owners.
  (6) Where at least two employees, etc. give notice of their intent to acquire the right to a service invention pursuant to paragraph (4), such right shall be jointly owned.
  (7) The head of a public research institute and employee, etc. may consult with each other on a plan to adjust the compensation to the employee, etc. for a service invention, in exchange for the employee, etc. partially bearing the expenses for retaining the right to a service invention, within the period referred to in paragraph (3).
  (8) The head of a public research institute may require an employee, etc. to reimburse expenses incurred in connection with the service invention (including taxes) after the date the right to such invention is deemed transferred to the employee, etc. pursuant to the former part of paragraph (5).
  [This Article Added on Apr. 20, 2021]

Article 17 (Operation of deliberative committee on service inventions) (1) An employer, etc. may establish and operate a deliberative committee on service inventions (hereinafter referred to as the "deliberative committee") to deliberate on the following matters regarding service inventions: <Amended on Jul. 30, 2013>
  1. Matters regarding the preparation, modification, and implementation of regulations on service inventions;
  2. Matters regarding the reconciliation of differences in opinions between an employee, etc. and the employer, etc. regarding rights, compensation, etc. for a service invention;
  3. Other necessary matters regarding service inventions.
  (2) The deliberative committee shall be comprised of an equal number of members representing an employer, etc. and employee, etc. (excluding executive officers of a corporation) respectively, and if necessary, experts in related fields may be commissioned as advisors. <Amended on Jul. 30, 2013>
  (3) Other matters necessary for the composition and operation of the deliberative committee shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Added on Jul. 30, 2013>
  [Title Amended on Jul. 30, 2013]

Article 18 (Mediation of disputes related to service inventions) (1) In any of the following cases, an employee, etc. may request his or her employer, etc. to organize a deliberative committee for deliberation: <Amended on Feb. 6, 2024>
  1. Where there are differences in opinions between the employee, etc. and employer, etc. as to whether an invention is a service invention;
  2. Where the employer, etc. asserts succession to rights to, or grant of an exclusive license to, an invention, other than a service invention, contrary to the intent of the employee, etc. in violation of Article 10 (3);
  3. Where the employer, etc. asserts succession to rights to, or grant of an exclusive license of, a service invention contrary to the intent of the employee, etc. in violation of Article 13 (2);
  4. Where the employer, etc. asserts a non-exclusive license in violation of the proviso of Article 10 (1), or 13 (3);
  5. Where there are differences in opinions on compensation regulations proposed by the employer, etc.;
  6. Where there are differences in opinions on procedures for consultation or consent with the employer, etc.;
  7. Where there are differences in opinions on the details of compensation, including the amount of compensation notified by the employer, etc. pursuant to Article 15 (4);
  8. Where the employer, etc. fails to pay compensation to the employee, etc. under Article 15 (2) through (4);
  9. Other cases where there are differences in opinions between the employer, etc. and employee, etc. with regard to the rights to, and compensation for, a service invention.
  (2) The rights under paragraph (1) shall be exercised within 30 days from the date any of the grounds under the subparagraphs of paragraph (1) arises; provided, in cases falling under paragraph (1) 7, the rights shall be exercised within 30 days from the date the employee, etc. receives notice.
  (3) Upon receipt of a request under paragraph (1), the employer, etc. shall organize a deliberative committee to deliberate on the relevant matter within 60 days. In such cases, the deliberative committee shall include at least one advisor who is an expert in the field related to service inventions.
  (4) The deliberative committee under paragraph (3) shall give written notice to the employer, etc. and employee, etc. of the results of deliberation without delay.
  (5) The Government may send an expert in the relevant field as an advisor under paragraph (3) at the request of the employer, etc., and matters necessary therefor shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (6) An employer, etc. or an employee, etc. dissatisfied with the results of deliberation by the deliberative committee under paragraph (3) may file an application for mediation with the committee for mediation of disputes over industrial property rights under Article 41. <Amended on May 18, 2015>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Jul. 30, 2013]

Article 19 (Duty of confidentiality) (1) An employee, etc. shall keep details of a service invention confidential until the employer, etc. files a patent application for the invention; provided, this shall not apply where it becomes conclusive that employer, etc. will not succeed to rights to the invention. <Amended on Jul. 30, 2013>
  (2) No person who participates or participated in the deliberative committee as an advisor under Article 18 (3) shall disclose to any other person the details of a service invention that he or she has learned in the course of performing his or her duties. <Added on Jul. 30, 2013>

SECTION 3 Creating Foundation for Invention Promotion

Article 20 Deleted. <Feb. 6, 2024>

Article 20-2 Deleted. <Feb. 6, 2024>

Article 20-3 Deleted. <Feb. 6, 2024>

Article 20-4 Deleted. <Feb. 6, 2024>

Article 20-5 Deleted. <Feb. 6, 2024>

Article 20-6 (Fact-finding survey on industrial property rights-related activities) (1) The Government shall conduct a fact-finding survey on universities, research institutes, enterprises, etc. every year in order to learn the overall status of intellectual property-related activities. <Amended on Oct. 20, 2020>
  (2) The Minister of Intellectual Property may entrust the fact-finding survey under paragraph (1) to an institution or organization prescribed by Presidential Decree that is deemed to have expertise in the research on intellectual property. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (3) Matters necessary for the details, methods, etc. of fact-finding surveys under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on May 18, 2015]

Article 20-7 Deleted. <Jul. 30, 2013>

Article 20-8 Deleted. <Feb. 6, 2024>

Article 21 Deleted. <May 18, 2015>

Article 22 Deleted. <May 18, 2015>

Article 23 (Regional intellectual property centers) (1) A regional intellectual property center may be established in each area in order to raise awareness of industrial property rights among regional small and medium enterprises and residents and to support the creation, protection and utilization of industrial property rights. <Amended on Mar. 30, 2011>
  (2) A regional intellectual property center under paragraph (1) (hereinafter referred to as a "regional intellectual property center") shall conduct the following business activities: <Amended on Jan. 27, 2010; Mar. 30, 2011>
  1. Provision of information or counseling on industrial property rights;
  2. Raising awareness of industrial property rights, including education and public relations thereon;
  3. Supporting the creation, protection and utilization of industrial property rights;
  4. Other support activities related to intellectual property rights.
  (3) A person who intends to establish a regional intellectual property center shall file for registration of the center with the Minister of Intellectual Property. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (4) A person who intends to be designated as an evaluation institute under paragraph (1) shall have specialized human resources, organizations, and facilities for evaluations prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Jul. 30, 2013; May 18, 2015; Jan. 3, 2023>
  (5) No person, other than a regional intellectual property center, shall use the name "regional intellectual property center".
  (6) The Government may subsidize the operation of regional intellectual property centers within the budget.
  (7) A regional intellectual property center may engage in profit-making business in order to finance the projects under paragraph (2). <Amended on Mar. 30, 2011>
  (8) A person who files for registration of a regional intellectual property center pursuant to paragraph (3) shall submit to the Minister of Intellectual Property a business plan for the relevant business year by one month before the date each business year begins and a business performance report for the relevant business year within three months from the date the business year ends. <Added on Mar. 30, 2011; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (9) Matters necessary for the registration procedures under paragraph (3) and other relevant matters shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Mar. 30, 2011>
  (10) The Minister of Intellectual Property may evaluate the performance, achievements, etc. of projects of a regional intellectual property center each year. In such cases, necessary matters regarding the procedures, methods, etc. for such evaluation shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Added on Mar. 30, 2011; Jul. 30, 2013; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (11) The Minister of Intellectual Property may warn a regional intellectual property center with poor business performance based on the results of an evaluation of business performance pursuant to paragraph (10) and suspend or reduce the financial support under paragraph (6). <Added on Mar. 30, 2011; Oct. 1, 2025>

Article 24 (Cancellation of registration of regional intellectual property center) (1) Where a regional intellectual property center falls under any of the following cases, the Minister of Intellectual Property may cancel the registration thereof or order the suspension of its business operations within a specified period of up to six months; provided, in cases falling under subparagraph 1, its registration shall be canceled: <Amended on Jul. 30, 2013; Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. When it is registered as a regional intellectual property center by fraud or other improper means;
  2. When it loses its capability to conduct the business activities under Article 23 (2);
  3. When it no longer meets the registration standards under Article 23 (4);
  4. When it fails to submit a business plan and a business performance report under Article 23 (8) within the period specified in that paragraph;
  5. When it receives at least two warnings under Article 23 (11) within the recent three years.
  (2) Detailed criteria for administrative dispositions under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree in consideration of the grounds therefor and the degree of violation. <Added on Jul. 30, 2013>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 30, 2011]

Article 24-2 (Intellectual property management certification for small and medium enterprises) (1) The Minister of Intellectual Property may grant intellectual property management certification (hereinafter referred to as "certification") to a small and medium enterprise that sets an example with strategic managerial activities in creating, protecting, and promoting the use of, industrial property rights. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) A small and medium enterprise that intends to obtain certification shall file an application for certification with the Minister of Intellectual Property. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (3) Upon receipt of an application for certification under paragraph (2), the Minister of Intellectual Property shall examine the small and medium enterprise seeking certification and grant a certificate with a validity period if it meets the standards for certification. <Added on Feb. 6, 2024; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (4) If a small and medium enterprise has obtained certification by fraud or other improper means, the Minister of Intellectual Property shall revoke the certification. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (5) If a small and medium enterprise that has obtained certification no longer meets the certification standards, the Minister of Intellectual Property may revoke the certification. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (6) The Minister of Intellectual Property may receive necessary expenses associated with certification from the relevant small and medium enterprise. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (7) Certification procedures and costs, certification standards, certification mark, designation of certifying agencies, the effective period of the certificate, and other matters necessary for certification shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on Jan. 27, 2016]

CHAPTER III SUPPORT FOR SECURING RIGHTS TO INVENTIONS

Article 25 (Prior art search) (1) Upon receipt of an application for an industrial property right, the Minister of Intellectual Property shall establish and enforce policy measures on a comprehensive search for prior art in the relevant field, in Korea and abroad, in order to examine and process the application promptly and accurately. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) The policy measures under paragraph (1) shall include the following:
  1. Collection and analysis of information on prior art;
  2. Outsourcing services for prior art;
  3. Other matters necessary for prior art search.

Article 26 (Establishment of patent management department) (1) The Minister of Intellectual Property shall establish and enforce supportive policy measures necessary for the efficient establishment and operation of patent management departments so as to efficiently address disputes, in Korea and abroad, over industrial property rights and secure the competitiveness of the industry by improving employers' patent management capabilities. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) The policy measures under paragraph (1) shall include the following:
  1. Providing information on the establishment of a patent management department;
  2. Education on industrial property rights for staff members of a patent management department;
  3. Other matters necessary for the establishment of a patent management department.

Article 26-2 (Patent counseling center of public patent attorneys) (1) The Minister of Intellectual Property shall establish a patent counseling center of public patent attorneys (hereinafter referred to as "counseling center") to provide pro bono patent legal services, such as counseling related to patent, to the socially disadvantaged. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) The counseling center shall execute the following business: <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Counseling and documentation support related to procedures for application, examination, registration, and adjudication of industrial property rights;
  2. Representation in matters to be filed with the Ministry of Intellectual Property or courts under Article 2 of the Patent Attorney Act;
  3. Reviewing applications for dispute mediation related to industrial property rights and supporting the preparation of tentative recommendations for settlement;
  4. Providing management consulting and legal advice on patent disputes;
  5. Holding briefings for industrial property rights and providing counseling;
  6. Providing legal service in connection with industrial property rights and other business prescribed by Presidential Decree that conforms to the purpose of operating the counseling center.
  (3) The counseling center shall provide services to any of the following persons: <Amended on May 18, 2015; Dec. 2, 2016>
  1. Eligible recipients of medical benefits under the National Basic Living Security Act;
  2. Persons who have rendered distinguished services to the State, and their families or bereaved families under Articles 4 and 5 of the Act on the Honorable Treatment of and Support for Persons of Distinguished Service to the State;
  3. Persons with disabilities registered under Article 32 (1) of the Act on Welfare of Persons with Disabilities;
  4. Students of schools under Article 2 of the Elementary and Secondary Education Act and Article 2 of the Higher Education Act (excluding students of special graduate schools);
  5. Small enterprises under Article 2 of the Framework Act on Small and Medium Enterprises;
  6. Other persons prescribed by Presidential Decree as particularly requiring counseling and support.
  (4) The Government may provide expenses necessary for the operation of the counseling center within its budgetary limits.
  (5) The Minister of Intellectual Property may entrust the operation of the counseling center to a corporation or organization prescribed by Presidential Decree, which has expertise in the field of industrial property rights. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (6) Matters necessary for organization, operation, scope of business, procedures, etc. of a counseling center shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on Jun. 8, 2010]

Article 27 (Subsidization of patent management expenses) (1) The Minister of Intellectual Property may take measures necessary to reduce expenses for filing patent applications and filing for registration so as to facilitate promptly securing rights to inventions made by an independent inventor or an employee, etc. as a result of his or her research and development, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) The Minister of Intellectual Property may take measures under paragraph (1) preferentially for kindergartens under subparagraph 2 of Article 2 of the Early Childhood Education Act, and students of schools under Article 2 of the Elementary and Secondary Education Act and Article 2 of the Higher Education Act, eligible recipients for medical benefits under Article 5 of the National Basic Living Security Act, and small enterprises not bigger than the size prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Dec. 12, 2016; Mar. 14, 2017; Oct. 1, 2025>

CHAPTER IV FACILITATING COMMERCIALIZATION OF INVENTIONS

Article 28 (Designation of evaluation institutes for inventions) (1) To facilitate the utilization, such as transfer, transaction and commercialization, of those falling under any item of subparagraph 11 of Article 2, the Minister of Intellectual Property may designate a national or public research institute, a government-funded research institute, a private research institute, or an institute specializing in evaluating inventions, etc., as an institute for evaluating inventions, etc. (hereinafter referred to as "evaluation institute"). <Amended on Jul. 30, 2013; Jan. 3, 2023; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) A person who intends to be designated as an evaluation institute under paragraph (1) shall have specialized human resources, organizations, and facilities for evaluations prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Jul. 30, 2013; May 18, 2015; Jan. 3, 2023>
  (3) A person who intends to have an invention, etc. evaluated may request an evaluation institute designated under paragraph (1) to evaluate the invention, etc. <Amended on Jul. 30, 2013; Jan. 3, 2023>
  (4) Upon receiving a request for evaluation under paragraph (3), an evaluation institute shall evaluate an invention, etc. and issue the person who has requested such evaluation an evaluation report (including one in the form of electronic documents defined in Article 2 of the Framework Act on Electronic Documents and Transactions) without delay. <Amended on Jan. 3, 2023>
  (5) The Minister of Intellectual Property may consult with the head of an evaluation institute on the following matters: <Amended on Jan. 3, 2023; Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Subject matter and scope of the evaluation of inventions, etc.;
  2. Financial support to the evaluation institute and evaluation fees;
  3. MOUs with the evaluation institute.
  (6) Matters necessary for the designation procedures under paragraphs (1) and (2) and other relevant matters shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [Title Amended on Jan. 3, 2023]

Article 29 (Business of evaluation institutes) (1) An evaluation institute may perform the following businesses:
  1. Evaluating inventions, etc.;
  2. Surveying and analyzing the demand for evaluation of inventions, etc.;
  3. Collecting, analyzing, providing, and distributing information on evaluation of inventions, etc., and establishing a related information network;
  4. Jointly utilizing and disseminating information on evaluation of inventions, etc.;
  5. Training experts in evaluation of inventions, etc.;
  6. Conducting research on the methods of evaluating inventions, etc.;
  7. Other matters necessary for evaluation of inventions, etc., which are prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (2) The Minister of Intellectual Property may grant a subsidy to an evaluation institute that performs any business prescribed in the subparagraphs of paragraph (1) to fully or partially cover the expenses incurred therein. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Jan. 3, 2023]

Article 29-2 (Special cases concerning investments in kind) Where a person intending to make an in-kind investment of those falling under any item of subparagraph 11 of Article 2 in a company undergoes an evaluation conducted by an evaluation institute, the details of such evaluation shall be deemed appraised by a certified appraiser under Article 299-2 or 422 of the Commercial Act. In such cases, a person in charge of evaluating inventions, etc. at the evaluation institute shall be deemed an appraiser for purposes of applying Articles 625, 630, and 635 of the Commercial Act.
  [This Article Added on Jan. 3, 2023]

Article 30 (Subsidization of evaluation fees) The Minister of Intellectual Property may fully or partially subsidize evaluation fees, within the budget, for a person whose invention, etc. has been evaluated by an evaluation institute pursuant to Article 28 (3) and (4). <Amended on Jan. 3, 2023; Oct. 1, 2025>

Article 31 (Revocation of designation of evaluation institute) (1) If an evaluation institute falls under any of the following, the Minister of Intellectual Property may revoke the designation thereof or order suspension of its business operations for a specified period not exceeding six months; provided, in cases falling under subparagraph 1, the designation shall be revoked: <Amended on Jul. 30, 2013; Jan. 3, 2023; Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Where it is designated by fraud or other improper means;
  2. Where it loses its capability to evaluate inventions, etc. under Article 28 (2) and (3);
  3. Where it evaluates inventions, etc., in violation of the criteria under Article 31-2.
  (2) Detailed criteria for administrative dispositions under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree in consideration of the grounds therefor and the degree of violation. <Added on Jul. 30, 2013>

Article 31-2 (Criteria for evaluations of inventions) (1) Criteria for evaluations of inventions, etc. to ensure fairness, objectivity, and reliability in such evaluations (hereinafter referred to as "evaluation criteria") shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (2) An evaluation institute shall comply with the evaluation criteria when evaluating inventions, etc.
  [This Article Added on Jan. 3, 2023]

Article 31-3 (Development and dissemination of techniques for evaluations of inventions) (1) The Minister of Intellectual Property shall develop and disseminate techniques for evaluating inventions, etc. (hereinafter referred to as "evaluation techniques") to create a market for evaluating inventions, etc. in an objective and professional manner. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) The Minister of Intellectual Property shall endeavor to disseminate evaluation techniques developed under paragraph (1) to evaluation institutes, public research institutes, financial companies, enterprises, etc. to facilitate the utilization of such evaluation techniques. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (3) Matters necessary for the development and dissemination of evaluation techniques, the facilitation of the utilization thereof, etc. shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on Jan. 3, 2023]

Article 31-4 (Study on evaluations of inventions) (1) The Minister of Intellectual Property may conduct a study on whether the relevant evaluation is performed in accordance with the evaluation criteria in a feasible manner (hereinafter referred to as "feasibility study") after an evaluation report is issued under Article 28 (4), ex officio or upon request of any of the following persons: <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. The State, a local government, a public institution under the Act on the Management of Public Institutions, or any other public organization prescribed by Presidential Decree (hereinafter referred to as the "State, etc.");
  2. An interested person prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (2) Where the Minister of Intellectual Property conducts a feasibility study, he or she shall give the relevant evaluation institute, the person who requests an evaluation of the relevant invention, etc., and the person who requests the feasibility study an opportunity to state his, her, or its opinion. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (3) Where the State, etc. makes a request on any ground prescribed by Presidential Decree, the Minister of Intellectual Property may provide the results of a feasibility study. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (4) A person who fails to submit an evaluation report or relevant data in violation of Article 31-5 (2) without good reason or submits a false report or data shall be subject to an administrative fine not exceeding 1.5 million won. <Added on Jan. 3, 2023>
  (5) Matters necessary for procedures, etc. for a feasibility study and a sample survey shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on Jan. 3, 2023]

Article 31-5 (Establishment and operation of evaluation information system) (1) The Minister of Intellectual Property may establish and operate a system for compiling and managing the results of evaluations of inventions, etc. performed by evaluation institutes and data related thereto (hereinafter referred to as "evaluation information system") in order to examine and manage evaluations of inventions, etc. efficiently and systematically. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) An evaluation institute shall submit an evaluation report prescribed in Article 28 (4) and related data necessary for establishing an evaluation information system to the Minister of Intellectual Property or the head of the evaluation management center specified in Article 31-6 (1), as prescribed by Presidential Decree; provided, where there exists any good reason prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as the protection of personal information, such report and data that exclude the part regarding the relevant reason may be submitted. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (3) When an evaluation institute receives a request for evaluating an invention, etc. under Article 28 (3), it shall notify the person who requests such evaluation of the fact that an evaluation report under paragraph (4) of that Article may be utilized on any ground prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as a feasibility study and a sample survey.
  (4) Where necessary for establishing and operating an evaluation information system, the Minister of Intellectual Property may request a relevant agency to submit data. In such cases, the agency so requested shall comply unless there is a compelling reason not to do so. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (5) Other matters necessary for establishing and operating an evaluation information system shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on Jan. 3, 2023]

Article 31-6 (Evaluation management center) (1) An evaluation management center shall be established to systematically perform business affairs for enhancing the reliability of evaluations, such as study and management of evaluations of inventions, etc.
  (2) The evaluation management center shall perform the following:
  1. Research, education, and publicity related to evaluations of inventions, etc.;
  2. Support for the formulation of the evaluation criteria;
  3. Development and dissemination of evaluation techniques;
  4. A feasibility study and a sample survey;
  5. Establishment and operation of an evaluation information system;
  6. Business affairs prescribed by Presidential Decree, which are incidental to those prescribed in subparagraphs 1 through 5.
  (3) The Government may fully or partially subsidize the establishment and operation of the evaluation management center or the performance of its business affairs.
  (4) Matters necessary for the organization and operation of the evaluation management center, its performance of business affairs, etc. shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on Jan. 3, 2023]

Article 31-7 (Confidentiality related to evaluations of inventions) (1) Evaluation institutes (including institutions which were designated as evaluation institutes) and the employees thereof (including persons who were employees of such evaluation institutes) shall not divulge any confidential information that they have learned in the course of conducting their duties for purposes other than the duties; provided, this shall not apply where this Act or any other statute or regulation provides otherwise.
  (2) A person who performs or has performed the duties related to an evaluation information system (including a person who is assigned the relevant duties under a service contract, etc., or his or her employee) shall not divulge any confidential information that he or she has learned in the course of his or her duties regarding the establishment, management, and utilization of the evaluation information system for purposes other than the duties; provided, this shall not apply where this Act or any other statute or regulation provides otherwise.
  [This Article Added on Jan. 3, 2023]

Article 32 (Assistance in commercialization of outstanding inventions) If an invention of an independent inventor or an employer is deemed outstanding based on the results of an evaluation of an invention, etc. conducted under Article 28 (4), the Minister of Intellectual Property may assist in the commercialization of the invention by providing financial support for the invention and facilitating the purchase thereof. <Amended on Jan. 3, 2023; Oct. 1, 2025>

Article 32-2 (Conducting program of purchasing and utilizing secured industrial property rights) (1) The Minister of Intellectual Property may conduct a program of purchasing and utilizing industrial property rights (hereinafter referred to as "program of purchasing and utilizing secured industrial property rights") which came into the possession of financial companies, etc. (referring to financial companies, etc. defined in subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Act on the Establishment of Korea Asset Management Corporation) (hereinafter referred to as "secured industrial property rights") as a result of any default by small and medium enterprises (referring to small and medium enterprises defined in Article 2 of the Framework Act on Small and Medium Enterprises) or middle-standing enterprises (referring to middle-standing enterprises defined in subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Special Act on the Promotion of Growth and the Strengthening of Competitiveness of Middle-Standing Enterprises), which took out a loan using industrial property rights as collateral. <Amended on Nov. 26, 2019; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) Methods, conditions, and other matters necessary for purchasing secured industrial property rights under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on Apr. 23, 2019]

Article 32-3 (Financing program of purchasing and utilizing secured industrial property rights) (1) In order to conduct the program of purchasing and utilizing secured industrial property rights, the Minister of Intellectual Property may make a contribution to any of the following institutions or organizations (hereinafter referred to as "responsible institution"): <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. The Korea Invention Promotion Association established under Article 52;
  2. The Korea Institute for Advancement of Technology under Article 38 of the Industrial Technology Innovation Promotion Act;
  3. Other institutions or organizations prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (2) Funds for the operation of the program of purchasing and utilizing secured intellectual property rights shall be raised from the following sources:
  1. Contributions from financial companies, etc.;
  2. Contribution from the Government;
  3. Proceeds from transactions of secured industrial property rights;
  4. Other financial resources prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (3) In order to ensure the efficient conduct of the program of purchasing and utilizing secured industrial property rights, a responsible institution may designate any institution or organization with expertise in the field of industrial property rights as a specialized agency and have it perform the following duties on its behalf; in such cases, the responsible institution may fully or partially subsidize the expenses incurred in performing the relevant duties: <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Purchase of secured industrial property rights;
  2. Management, disposal, grant of license, and other uses of secured industrial property rights that have been purchased;
  3. Other duties deemed by the Minister of Intellectual Property to be necessary for operating the business.
  (4) Except as provided in paragraphs (1) and (3), matters necessary for conducting the program of purchasing and utilizing secured industrial property rights shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on Apr. 23, 2019]

Article 33 Deleted. <Mar. 18, 2009>

Article 34 (Establishment of the Korea Industrial Property Business Support Center) (1) A Korea Industrial Property Business Support Center (hereinafter referred to as the "Business Support Center") shall be established to support the commercialization or utilization of technology related to any of the following inventions, etc. (hereafter in this Article referred to as "patent technology") and of trademarks: <Amended on Jan. 3, 2023>
  1. An invention for which an application is filed in the Republic of Korea or a foreign country or which is registered;
  2. A trade secret;
  3. A layout-design.
  (2) The Business Support Center shall conduct the following business activities: <Amended on Jan. 30, 2009; Mar. 18, 2009; Jul. 30, 2013; Jan. 3, 2023>
  1. Operation of a permanent market and an Internet market for patent technology for referral or brokerage of transfer, sale, or purchase of patent technology and trademarks;
  2. Referral or brokerage of grant of a license or a right to use for an industrial property right (including where an industrial property right holder grants the execution or use of such license or such right to the Business Support Center, and it, then, grants a sub-license or a right to use therefor to a third party; in such cases, the Business Support Center shall pay the royalties that it has received from the third party to the industrial property right holder in compliance with the extent and procedures stipulated in the agreement concluded with the industrial property right holder);
  3. Survey, analysis, and evaluation of the demand for referral or brokerage of patent technology and trademarks;
  4. Collection, analysis, and provision of information related to referral or brokerage of patent technology and trademarks;
  5. Establishment of a cooperative system with institutions related to technology transfer, such as the Korea Institute for Advancement of Technology under Article 38 of the Industrial Technology Innovation Promotion Act;
  6. Other activities necessary to support the commercialization of patent technology and to facilitate referral or brokerage of patent technology.
  (3) The Government may make a contribution to help cover all or part of the expenses for the establishment and operation of the Business Support Center or the conduct of its business activities. <Amended on Jan. 3, 2023>
  (4) The organization, functions, and operation of the Business Support Center, the Government's contribution, and other necessary matters shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Jan. 3, 2023>
  [Title Amended on Jan. 3, 2023]

Article 35 (Subsidization of production of prototype) The Government may fully or partially subsidize, within the budget, the production of a prototype of an invention deemed outstanding based on the results of an evaluation of an invention, etc. conducted pursuant to Article 28 (4). <Amended on Jan. 3, 2023>

Article 36 Deleted. <Feb. 6, 2024>

Article 37 Deleted. <Feb. 6, 2024>

Article 38 (Request for modification to various specifications) If an invention registered as an industrial property right is excluded from products eligible for purchase by the State, local governments, public institutions and other similar entities under Article 4 of the Act on the Management of Public Institutions because its specifications are different from conventional specifications, the Minister of Intellectual Property may request the head of the relevant administrative agency in charge of the management of the relevant specifications to modify or supplement the specifications so that the products based on the invention become eligible for such purchase. <Amended on Mar. 18, 2009; Oct. 1, 2025>

Article 39 (Preferential purchase of outstanding invention) When an end-user institution defined in subparagraph 5 of Article 2 of the Government Procurement Act purchases goods, it may preferentially purchase outstanding inventions made by small and medium enterprises (referring to small and medium business under Article 2 of the Framework Act on Small and Medium Enterprises) recommended by the Minister of Intellectual Property. <Amended on Mar. 18, 2009; Jul. 30, 2013; Jan. 27, 2016; Oct. 1, 2025>

Article 39-2 (Support for publicity for outstanding invention) The Minister of Intellectual Property may provide support for publicity for inventions falling under any of the following: <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Outstanding inventions of small and medium enterprises under Article 39;
  2. Inventions recognized for public interest, such as preventing criminal harms and enhancing occupational safety, in compliance with the procedures prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on Dec. 2, 2016]

Article 40 (Tax support) The Government may provide tax support for income or expenses arising from the promotion of inventions, filing of an application for industrial property rights, filing for registration of such rights, or transfer or licensing of industrial property rights, as provided in the Act on Restriction on Special Cases concerning Taxation.

CHAPTER IV-2 FOSTERING OF INDUSTRIAL PROPERTY RIGHTS SERVICE BUSINESS

Article 40-2 (Establishment and implementation of fostering policies) (1) The Minister of Intellectual Property shall establish and implement polices necessary to foster the industrial property rights service industry (hereinafter referred to as "fostering policies") annually. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) Fostering polices shall include the following matters:
  1. Establishment of the foundation necessary to foster the industrial property rights service industry;
  2. Enhancement of the competitiveness of the industrial property rights service industry;
  3. Facilitation of the utilization of the industrial property rights service industry and support for business start-up;
  4. Other matters necessary for fostering the industrial property rights service industry.
  [This Article Added on Jul. 30, 2013]

Article 40-3 (Enhancement of competitiveness of industrial property rights service industry) (1) The Minister of Intellectual Property may perform the following duties to enhance the competitiveness of the industrial property rights service industry: <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Nurturing human resources to enhance the expertise of the industrial property rights service industry;
  2. Facilitating international cooperation on, and overseas expansion of, the industrial property rights service industry;
  3. Other affairs necessary to enhance the competitiveness of the industrial property rights service industry.
  (2) The Minister of Intellectual Property may designate a specialized institution or organization to conduct programs under paragraph (1) on his or her behalf. In such cases, the associated expenses may be fully or partially subsidized. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (3) The criteria and procedures for the designation of a specialized institution or organization referred to in paragraph (2) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (4) Where a specialized institution or organization designated pursuant to paragraph (2) falls under any of the following cases, the Minister of Intellectual Property may revoke the designation or order the suspension of its business affairs or programs for a specified period not exceeding six months; provided, in cases falling under subparagraph 1, the designation shall be revoked: <Amended on Jan. 3, 2023; Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Where it is designated by fraud or other improper means;
  2. Where it loses its capability to conduct the affairs or programs;
  3. Where it fails to meet the criteria for designation under paragraph (3).
  (5) Detailed criteria for administrative dispositions made under paragraph (4) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree in consideration of the grounds therefor and the severity of violation. <Added on Jan. 3, 2023>
  [This Article Added on Jul. 30, 2013]

Article 40-4 (Facilitation of utilization of industrial property rights service industry and support for business start-up) The Minister of Intellectual Property may perform the following to facilitate the utilization of the industrial property rights service industry and to revitalize business start-up in the industry: <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Public relations to raise awareness of the industrial property rights service industry;
  2. Providing information, counseling, and holding fairs and exhibitions related to business start-up;
  3. Selecting and rewarding outstanding industrial property rights service providers and exemplary start-up cases;
  4. Other business affairs necessary to facilitate the utilization of the industrial property rights service industry and encourage business start-up.
  [This Article Added on Jul. 30, 2013]

Article 40-5 (Fact-finding surveys on industrial property rights service industry) (1) The Minister of Intellectual Property may conduct a fact-finding survey on the industrial property rights service industry for a period of up to three years, to efficiently establish and implement fostering policies for the industry. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) To conduct a fact-finding survey pursuant to paragraph (1), the Minister of Intellectual Property may request an industrial property rights service provider to submit data prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as the personnel status and revenues, or to state opinions. In such cases, the industrial property rights service provider shall cooperate therein, unless there is a compelling reason not to do so, such as the request made under the former part constituting a trade secret. <Amended on Oct. 20, 2020; Jan. 3, 2023; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (3) The frequency, method, items, etc. of fact-finding surveys under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on Jul. 30, 2013]
  [Title Amended on May 18, 2015]

Article 40-6 (Establishment and operation of association) (1) An industrial property rights service provider may establish an association relating to the industrial property rights service industry (hereinafter referred to as "association") to promote the sound development of such business and the common interest of industrial property rights service providers.
  (2) The Association shall be a juristic person.
  (3) The Association shall conduct the following business affairs: <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Conducting research on relevant systems for the development of the industrial property rights service industry, and making suggestions for improvement thereof;
  2. Managing the current status and statistics regarding industrial property rights service providers;
  3. Collecting, analyzing, and providing information on the industrial property rights service industry;
  4. Business affairs entrusted by the Minister of Intellectual Property for fostering the industrial property rights service industry;
  5. Other business affairs necessary to achieve the objectives of establishing the association.
  (4) Except as provided in this Act, the provisions of the Civil Act governing incorporated associations shall apply mutatis mutandis to the association.
  [This Article Added on Jul. 30, 2013]

Article 40-7 (Specialized companies for industrial property rights services) (1) In order to facilitate the use of industrial property rights services, the Minister of Intellectual Property may designate a company that professionally provides industrial property rights services (excluding subparagraph 9 (b) of Article 2; hereafter the same shall apply in this Article) and satisfies the standards for human resources, facilities, etc., prescribed by Presidential Decree, as a specialized company for industrial property rights services (hereinafter referred to as "specialized company"). <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) Where a specialized company falls under any of the following, the Minister of Intellectual Property may revoke the designation; provided, he or she shall revoke the designation in cases falling under subparagraph 1: <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Where it is designated by fraud or other improper means;
  2. Where it has no track record of providing industrial property rights services for two years after designation;
  3. Where it no longer meets the designation standards under paragraph (1).
  (3) The Government may provide specialized companies with necessary support.
  (4) Matters necessary for the designation under paragraph (1), revocation of designation under paragraph (2), support by the Government under paragraph (3), etc., shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on May 18, 2015]

CHAPTER V MEDIATION OF DISPUTES OVER INDUSTRIAL PROPERTY RIGHTS AND FACILITATION OF SHARING OF TECHNOLOGY

Article 41 (Committee for mediation of disputes over industrial property rights) (1) A committee for mediation of disputes over industrial property rights (hereinafter referred to as the "Committee") shall be established in order to examine and mediate disputes over the following matters (hereinafter referred to as "disputes"): <Amended on Jun. 8, 2010; May 18, 2015; Feb. 4, 2020; Jan. 30, 2024>
  1. Industrial property rights (including applications for industrial property rights);
  2. Service inventions;
  3. Trade secrets;
  4. An act of unfair competition defined in subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Unfair Competition Prevention and Trade Secret Protection Act (hereinafter referred to as "act of unfair competition");
  5. Layout-design rights defined in subparagraph 5 of Article 2 of the Act on the Layout-Designs of Semiconductor Integrated Circuits, exclusive licenses under Article 11 of that Act, and non-exclusive licenses under Article 12 of that Act;
  6. Other matters subject to deliberation by the Committee under other statutes or regulations.
  (2) The Committee shall be comprised of not less than 15 but not more than 100 members for mediation (hereinafter referred to as "mediators"), including one chairperson. <Amended on Jan. 27, 2010; Jun. 8, 2010; Feb. 4, 2020>
  (3) Mediators of the Committee shall be commissioned by the Minister of Intellectual Property, from among the following persons, and the chairperson shall be appointed by the Minister of Intellectual Property, from among the mediators: <Amended on Jan. 27, 2010; Jun. 8, 2010; May 18, 2015; Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Public officials ranked Grade III or members of the Senior Executive Service, among public officials of the Ministry of Intellectual Property;
  2. Persons who currently serve as a judge or a public prosecutor;
  3. Persons who hold a license as attorney-at-law or patent attorney;
  4. Associate professors or higher-ranked professors at a college;
  5. Persons recommended by non-profit, non-governmental organizations under Article 2 of the Assistance for Non-Profit, Non-Governmental Organizations Act;
  6. Other persons with extensive knowledge of and experience in the matters referred to in the subparagraphs of paragraph (1).
  (4) The term of office of each mediator shall be three years; provided, the term of office of mediators who fall under paragraph (3) 1 or 2 shall coincide with their respective term of office for the position entitling them to serve on the Committee.
  (5) A vacancy for a mediator shall be filled by a substitute commissioned in accordance with paragraph (3), and his or her term of office shall be the remaining term of his or her predecessor; provided, if the number of mediators is not less than 15 persons, a substitute may not be commissioned.
  (6) A secretariat shall be established in the Korea Institute for Intellectual Property Protection Agency under Article 55-2 (1) to support the Committee in the conduct of business affairs. <Added on Feb. 4, 2020>

Article 41-2 (Exclusion of, challenge to, or recusal of, mediators) (1) A mediator shall be excluded from deliberation on and mediation of the relevant dispute mediation case (hereafter in this Article referred to as "case") in any of the following circumstances:
  1. Where the mediator or the spouse or former spouse of the member is a party to the relevant case or is a joint interest holder or obligor in the case;
  2. Where the mediator is a current or former relative of a party to the relevant case;
  3. Where the mediator has directly engaged in an examination, adjudication or trial in connection with the relevant case;
  4. Where the mediator is or was involved in the relevant case as a witness, appraiser or agent of a party to the relevant case;
  5. Where the mediator has direct interests in the relevant case.
  (2) A party to a dispute may file an application for challenge with the Committee if there are circumstances that make it difficult to expect a fair deliberation and mediation from a mediator; in such cases, where the Committee deems that the application for a challenge is well-grounded, it shall decide to accept such challenge against the relevant mediator.
  (3) Where a mediator falls under paragraph (1) or (2), he or she may recuse himself or herself from deliberation on or mediation of the relevant case.
  [This Article Added on Jun. 8, 2010]

Article 41-3 (Dismissal of mediators) The Minister of Intellectual Property may dismiss a mediator in any of the following circumstances: <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Where the mediator becomes unable to perform his or her duties due to mental or physical disabilities;
  2. Where he or she commits irregularities related to his or her duties;
  3. Where he or she is deemed unfit as a mediator due to neglect of duty, loss of dignity or for other similar reasons;
  4. Where he or she fails to recuse himself or herself despite falling under any subparagraph of Article 41-2 (1);
  5. Where it is deemed impractical for him or her to perform the relevant duties.
  [This Article Added on Feb. 4, 2020]

Article 42 (Mediation panel) In order to efficiently mediate disputes, the Committee shall have a mediation panel comprised of up to three mediators, one of whom shall be an attorney-at-law or a patent attorney. <Amended on Jun. 8, 2010; Feb. 4, 2020>

Article 43 (Application for mediation) (1) A person who intends to have a dispute mediated may file an application for mediation with the Committee, stating the purpose and ground for the application. <Amended on Jun. 8, 2010>
  (2) The mediation of a dispute under paragraph (1) shall be conducted by a mediation panel under Article 42.
  (3) The Committee shall complete mediation within three months from the filing date of an application for mediation; provided, where reasonable grounds are deemed to exist, the period may be extended up to three times by not more than one month each time; and in such cases, the extended period and grounds therefor shall be notified to the parties to the mediation. <Amended on Jun. 8, 2010; Mar. 21, 2017>
  (4) If the period under paragraph (3) lapses, it shall be deemed that mediation has failed.
  (5) Where an application for mediation is filed, the respondent shall comply with the mediation in good faith. <Added on Mar. 21>

Article 43-2 (Eligible applicant for mediation) (1) Eligibility to apply for mediation of a dispute pursuant to Article 43 (1) shall be limited to any of the following persons; provided, where a person without any address or business office in Korea may apply for mediation only through an agent who has an address or business office in Korea: <Amended on May 18, 2015; Feb. 4, 2020; Jan. 30, 2024>
  1. An applicant for an industrial property right;
  2. A titleholder;
  3. A licensee;
  4. A holder of a right to use;
  5. An employee-inventor;
  6. A person holding trade secrets;
  7. Parties to a dispute over an act of unfair competition;
  7-2. A holder of a layout-design right under Article 8 of the Act on the Layout-Designs of Semiconductor Integrated Circuits, an exclusive licensee under Article 11 (2) of that Act, and a non-exclusive licensee under Article 12 (2) of that Act;
  8. Other persons who have a direct interest in the exercise of the relevant rights or in service inventions, trade secrets, or an act of unfair competition.
  (2) Among persons under paragraph (1), a minor, a person under adult guardianship, or a person under limited guardianship may apply for mediation only through his or her legal representative. <Amended on Jul. 30, 2013>
  [This Article Added on Jun. 8, 2010]

Article 44 (Matters excluded from subject matter of application for mediation) A case seeking a mere determination as to whether an industrial property right is invalid or canceled or confirmation of the scope of a right, among disputes, shall not be eligible for an application for mediation.

Article 45 (Summons) (1) The Committee may request the parties, their representatives or interested persons to appear if necessary for the dispute mediation. <Amended on Jun. 8, 2010; Feb. 4, 2020>
  (2) If a party to the mediation fails to comply with a summons under paragraph (1) twice, without good cause, it shall be deemed that a mediation has failed. <Amended on Mar. 21, 2017>

Article 45-2 (Fact-finding surveys) The Committee may conduct investigations or request disputing parties to submit relevant materials, where necessary to ascertain facts underlying a dispute subject to mediation.
  [This Article Added on Feb. 4, 2020]

Article 46 (Completion of mediation) (1) Mediation shall be complete when matters agreed upon between parties are recorded in a protocol.
  (2) The protocol under paragraph (1) shall be as effective as judicial compromise; provided, this shall not apply to matters of which neither party can dispose at will.

Article 46-2 (Refusal and suspension of mediation) (1) The Committee may refuse or suspend mediation in any of the following cases:
  1. Where one party to a dispute refuses mediation;
  2. Where either party to the dispute has filed a suit with the court or has done so after an application for mediation is filed;
  3. Cases prescribed by Presidential Decree as having no actual benefit from mediation, such as where the details of the application are clearly recognized by the relevant statutes or regulations or objective data.
  (2) Where any ground for refusal or suspension of mediation under paragraph (1) arises, the Committee shall notify the parties to the dispute thereof, in writing.
  [This Article Added on Jun. 8, 2010]

Article 47 (Interruption of extinctive prescription) (1) An application for mediation shall have the effect of interrupting extinctive prescription.
  (2) If successful mediation has not been made and if no lawsuit is filed within one month from the date the failure in mediation becomes final and conclusive, extinctive prescription shall not be interrupted.

Article 48 (Composition of Committee) The composition and operation of the Committee and a mediation panel under Article 42, methods and procedures for the mediation of disputes, the handling of mediation affairs, and other necessary matters shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Feb. 4, 2020>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Jun. 8, 2010]

Article 49 (Subsidization of expenses) The Government may subsidize the operation of the Committee, within the budget. <Amended on Jun. 8, 2010>

Article 49-2 (Prohibition of divulgence of confidential information) No person who is or was a mediator of the Committee shall divulge any confidential information that he or she has learned in the course of performing his or her duties. <Amended on Jun. 8, 2010; Feb. 4, 2020>
  [This Article Added on Aug. 3, 2007]

Article 49-3 (Special cases concerning linkage between trial and mediation) (1) Where a decision is made to refer a case to the Committee pursuant to Article 164-2 of the Patent Act, Article 33 of the Utility Model Act, Article 152-2 of the Design Protection Act, or Article 151-2 of the Trademark Act, it shall be deemed that an application under Article 43 (1) has been filed on the date such case is referred to the Committee.
  (2) Where an application for mediation has been filed for the case under paragraph (1) and where it is deemed necessary by the relevant presiding judge and agreed upon by the parties, all or part of the relevant collegiate body of judges may become members of a mediation panel under Article 42.
  [This Article Added on Aug. 17, 2011]

Article 50 (Facilitation of joint ownership and reciprocal licensing of industrial property rights) (1) The Minister of Intellectual Property shall establish and enforce a supportive policy necessary for facilitating joint ownership of industrial property rights or reciprocal grant of non-exclusive licenses in such industrial property rights (hereinafter referred to as "joint ownership or reciprocal licensing of industrial property rights") based on an agreement on joint ownership or reciprocal licensing of industrial property rights signed between employers, etc. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) The supportive policy under paragraph (1) shall include the following matters:
  1. Providing information, in Korea and abroad, regarding joint ownership and reciprocal licensing of industrial property rights;
  2. Holding briefings for facilitating joint ownership and reciprocal licensing of industrial property rights;
  3. Other matters necessary for facilitating joint ownership and reciprocal licensing of industrial property rights.
  (3) The Minister of Intellectual Property may request the Minister of Trade, Industry and Resources or the Chairperson of the Korea Invention Promotion Association under Article 52 to grant a subsidy for expenses incurred to employers, etc. who have entered into an agreement on joint ownership or reciprocal licensing of industrial property rights in accordance with paragraph (1) when they jointly develop technology for the technical area subject to the joint ownership or reciprocal licensing of industrial property rights, primarily from the fund under Article 55, the funds for industrial technology development projects under Article 11 (2) of the Industrial Technology Innovation Promotion Act, the fund for business start-up and promotion of small and medium enterprises and startups under Article 63 of the Small and Medium Enterprises Promotion Act, etc. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; May 21, 2009; Jan. 27, 2010; Mar. 23, 2013; Dec. 31, 2018; Oct. 1, 2025>

Article 50-2 (Protection of industrial property rights) (1) The Government may engage in the business of protecting industrial property rights, as prescribed by Presidential Decree, to enhance technical competitiveness of industries and establish a fair trade system.
  (2) The Minister of Intellectual Property may designate a specialized institution or organization to conduct the business under paragraph (1) on his or her behalf. In such cases, expenses incurred in conducting such programs may be fully or partially subsidized. <Amended on Jul. 30, 2013; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (3) The criteria and procedures for designating a specialized institution or organization under paragraph (2) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Added on Jul. 30, 2013>
  (4) Article 40-3 (4) and (5) shall apply mutatis mutandis to revocation of designation of the specialized institution or organization under paragraph (2) or suspension of its business operations. <Added on Jul. 30, 2013; Jan. 3, 2023>
  [This Article Added on Jan. 27, 2010]

Article 50-3 (Overseas industrial property rights center) (1) An overseas industrial property rights center may be established to assist exporters in securing, utilizing, and protecting industrial property rights abroad.
  (2) An overseas industrial property rights center under paragraph (1) (hereafter referred to as "overseas industrial property rights center" in this Article) shall conduct the following activities:
  1. Assisting exporters in applying for, registering, and utilizing industrial property rights abroad;
  2. Assisting exporters in dealing with disputes over industrial property rights abroad;
  3. Assisting exporters in protecting trade secrets abroad;
  4. Sharing and disseminating information on protection of industrial property rights abroad;
  5. Collecting relevant overseas data to support the application and registration of industrial property rights;
  6. Establishing a cooperative network for protection of industrial property rights abroad;
  7. Survey and public relations activities for systems, statistics, and demands for protection of industrial property rights abroad;
  8. Other matters necessary for exporters to secure, utilize, and protect industrial property rights abroad.
  (3) The Government may provide financial support necessary for conducting business activities, within the budget, to a person who operates an overseas industrial property rights center.
  (4) An overseas industrial property rights center may engage in profit-making business to the extent necessary to perform its duties under paragraph (2). <Amended on Feb. 6, 2024>
  [This Article Added on Jan. 27, 2010]

Article 50-4 (Management and operation of mutual aid program related to intellectual property rights) The Minister of Intellectual Property may manage and operate mutual aid programs for small and medium enterprises under Article 2 of the Framework Act on Small and Medium Enterprises and middle-standing enterprises under subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Special Act on the Promotion of Growth and the Strengthening of Competitiveness of Middle-Standing Enterprises to hedge and mitigate financial risks which may be caused by expenses related to intellectual property right, including expenses for international application of industrial property right and domestic or international litigation related to intellectual property right (hereinafter referred to as the "patent mutual aid program"). <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  [This Article Added on Nov. 28, 2017]

Article 50-5 (Entrusting or funding patent mutual aid program) (1) The Minister of Intellectual Property may entrust operation of mutual aid programs to any of the following agencies or organizations to efficiently operate the patent mutual aid program: <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. The Korea Federation of SMEs under the Small and Medium Enterprise Cooperatives Act;
  2. Other agencies or organizations prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (2) Funds for the operation of the patent mutual aid program shall be raised from the following financial resources:
  1. Mutual savings deposited by members;
  2. Contributions from enterprises, agencies or organizations related to intellectual property rights, and others;
  3. Contributions or subsidies from the Government to cover initial operating expenses;
  4. Other financial resources prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (3) Except as provided in paragraphs (1) and (2), matters necessary to operate the patent mutual aid program shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on Nov. 28, 2017]

Article 50-6 (Accumulation of reserves) (1) The Minister of Intellectual Property or an agency or organization entrusted with a patent mutual aid program pursuant to Article 50-5 shall set aside reserves to cover future refunds for each accounting period, and shall accumulate and operate them separately. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) Matters necessary for the accumulation and operation of reserves under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on Jun. 20, 2023]

Article 51 (Korea Institute of Intellectual Property) (1) The Government shall establish Korea Institute of Intellectual Property (hereinafter referred to as "Institute") in order to prepare efficient schemes for dealing with disputes, in Korea and abroad, related to intellectual property rights, analyze domestic and overseas trends of intellectual property rights, and conduct research on the field of new intellectual property rights. <Amended on Mar. 22, 2013>
  (2) The Institute shall be a corporation. <Added on Mar. 22, 2013>
  (3) The Institute shall be established by registering its incorporation at the seat of its main office. <Added on Mar. 22, 2013>
  (4) The Institute shall perform the following affairs as prescribed by its articles of incorporation: <Added on Mar. 22, 2013>
  1. Survey and research on intellectual property in Korea and abroad;
  2. International cooperation and exchange with regard to intellectual property in Korea and abroad;
  3. Projects for the promotion of awareness of intellectual property in Korea and abroad, information collection, operation of a special intellectual property library, etc.;
  4. Undertaking of research contracts issued by the Government, domestic and foreign public institutions or private organizations, or corporations, etc. or conducting joint research with them;
  5. Policy advice or recommendation on intellectual property and intellectual property rights;
  6. Other projects incidental to those referred to in subparagraphs 1 through 5, and projects deemed by the Government to serve the purpose of the establishment prescribed in paragraph (1).
  (5) The Government shall establish and enforce a supporting policy necessary for the Institute under paragraph (1). <Amended on Mar. 22, 2013>
  (6) The policy under paragraph (5) shall include the following matters: <Amended on Mar. 22, 2013>
  1. Subsidization of project costs and operating expenses;
  2. Sending public officials for research on intellectual property rights;
  3. Other matters necessary for research on intellectual property.
  (7) Except as provided in this Act, the provisions of the Civil Act governing incorporated foundations shall apply mutatis mutandis to the Institute. <Added on Mar. 22, 2013>
  (8) The Minister of Intellectual Property shall guide and supervise the Institute in its conduct of business affairs. <Added on Mar. 22, 2013; Oct. 1, 2025>
  [Title Amended on Mar. 22, 2013]

CHAPTER VI KOREA INVENTION PROMOTION ASSOCIATION

Article 52 (Establishment of Korea Invention Promotion Association) (1) The Korea Invention Promotion Association shall be established in order to promote projects for promotion of invention systematically and efficiently and to conduct programs for improving inventors' interests.
  (2) The Korea Invention Promotion Association shall be a corporation.
  (3) The Korea Invention Promotion Association shall be duly established by registering the establishment at the location of its principal place of business.
  (4) Where necessary, the Korea Invention Promotion Association may establish branches in necessary places at home and abroad as prescribed by the articles of association.
  (5) No person, other than the Korea Invention Promotion Association, shall use the name "Korea Invention Promotion Association".
  (6) Except as provided in this Act, the provisions governing incorporated foundations in the Civil Act shall apply mutatis mutandis to the Korea Invention Promotion Association.

Article 53 (Business activities) (1) The Korea Invention Promotion Association shall conduct the following business activities: <Amended on Jul. 30, 2013; Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Survey and research on promotion of inventions and informatization;
  2. Collection, analysis, and dissemination of technical information and data on industrial property rights;
  3. Training of talents relating to industrial property rights and operation of educational facilities;
  4. Fostering of education, research, and teachers relating to invention;
  5. Exhibitions, events, and international exchanges and cooperation to promote inventions;
  6. Support for the creation, protection, and utilization of industrial property rights through regional intellectual property centers;
  7. Facilitation of evaluation and commercialization of patent technology;
  8. Projects entrusted by the Minister of Intellectual Property for promotion of inventions;
  9. Other business activities stipulated by its articles of association.
  (2) The Korea Invention Promotion Association may engage in a profit-making business in order to finance the business activities under paragraph (1).
  (3) For promotion of inventions, the Government may fully or partially subsidize the project costs and operating expenses of the Korea Invention Promotion Association within the budget.

Article 54 (Guidance and supervision) The Minister of Intellectual Property shall guide and supervise the Korea Invention Promotion Association in its conduct of business affairs. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>

Article 55 (Fund-raising) (1) The Korea Invention Promotion Association may raise and manage the fund for efficient support to invention promotion projects under this Act (hereinafter referred to as the "Fund").
  (2) The Fund shall be raised from the following sources:
  1. Earnings from profit-making business under Article 53 (2);
  2. Contributions and donations from employers, etc.;
  3. Borrowings;
  4. Proceeds from the operation of the Fund;
  5. Other revenues prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (3) The Fund shall be used for the following business activities: <Amended on Jan. 3, 2023; Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Promoting inventive activities, such as holding events for encouraging inventions;
  2. Subsidizing production of prototypes of outstanding inventions;
  3. Subsidizing evaluations of inventions, etc.;
  4. Subsidizing transfer of inventions, grant of licenses, subsidizing funds for business start-up, and subsidizing commercialization;
  5. Facilitating utilization of the service invention system;
  6. Encouraging application and registration of industrial property rights in Korea and abroad;
  7. Encouraging students' inventions;
  8. Survey on and analysis of industrial property rights;
  9. Survey, research, and development of systems for industrial property rights;
  10. Assistance in pro-bono patent services for inventors who are students or social benefit recipients;
  11. Assistance in providing credit guarantees in granting subsidies for commercialization of industrial property rights;
  12. Other business activities deemed by the Chairperson of the Korea Invention Promotion Association to be necessary for promotion of inventions.

CHAPTER VI-2 KOREA INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY PROTECTION AGENCY

Article 55-2 (Establishment of Korea Intellectual Property Protection Agency) (1) The Korea Intellectual Property Protection Agency (hereinafter referred to as the "Protection Agency") shall be established to provide support for the protection of intellectual property.
  (2) The Protection Agency shall be a corporation.
  (3) The Protection Agency shall be established by registering such establishment at the location of its main office.
  (4) A person other than the Protection Agency shall not use the name of the Korea Intellectual Property Protection Agency.
  (5) Except as provided in this Act, the provisions governing incorporated foundations under the Civil Act shall apply mutatis mutandis to the Protection Agency.
  [This Article Added on Feb. 4, 2020]

Article 55-3 (Business affairs of Protection Agency) (1) The Protection Agency shall perform the following business affairs; provided, the business affairs of the Korea Copyright Protection Agency under subparagraphs 1 through 6 of Article 122-5 of the Copyright Act shall be excluded herefrom: <Amended on Nov. 15, 2022; Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Research and study on the protection of intellectual property in Korea and abroad;
  2. Creation of infrastructure for the protection of intellectual property in Korea and abroad and education and publicity thereof;
  3. International cooperation for the protection of intellectual property in Korea and abroad;
  4. Prevention of disputes and support for responses for the protection of intellectual property in Korea and abroad;
  5. Provision of support for law enforcement affairs against unfair competition practices and infringements on trademarks and exclusive licenses under subparagraph 38 of Article 5 of the Act on the Persons Performing the Duties of Judicial Police Officers and the Scope of Their Duties;
  5-2. Provision of support for law enforcement affairs against an act of infringement of patent rights or exclusive licenses, unfair competition practices, the acquisition, use, or disclosure of trade secrets, and infringement of design rights or exclusive licenses under subparagraph 38-2 of Article 5 of the Act on the Persons Performing the Duties of Judicial Police Officers and the Scope of Their Duties;
  6. Supporting the operation of the Committee;
  7. Business affairs entrusted by the Minister of Intellectual Property for the protection of intellectual property in Korea and abroad;
  8. Other business affairs prescribed by Presidential Decree as necessary to attain the establishment objectives of the Protection Agency.
  (2) The Protection Agency may engage in a profit-making business to raise funds necessary to perform its functions specified in paragraph (1).
  (3) The Government may fully or partially subsidize the project costs and operating expenses of the Protection Agency within the budget.
  [This Article Added on Feb. 4, 2020]

Article 55-4 (Guidance and supervision for Protection Agency) The Minister of Intellectual Property shall guide and supervise the Protection Agency in its conduct of business affairs. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  [This Article Added on Feb. 4, 2020]

CHAPTER VI-3 Deleted.

Article 55-5 Deleted. <Feb. 6, 2024>

Article 55-6 Deleted. <Feb. 6, 2024>

Article 55-7 Deleted. <Feb. 6, 2024>

CHAPTER VII SUPPLEMENTARY PROVISIONS

Article 55-8 (Submission of data) (1) Upon receipt of a request from a party in a lawsuit over compensation for a service invention, the court may order the other party to submit data necessary for calculating the amount of compensation for such invention; provided, this shall not apply where the person in possession of the data has good reasons for refusing to submit them.
  (2) Where a person in possession of the data argues that he or she has good reasons for refusing to submit them under paragraph (1), the court may order him or her to present them to determine the validity of the argument. In such cases, the court shall not allow any other person to access the data.
  (3) Where the data required to be submitted pursuant to paragraph (1) fall under trade secrets but are absolutely necessary for calculating the amount of compensation for a service invention, it shall be deemed that good reasons under the proviso of paragraph (1) do not exist. In such cases, the court shall designate the scope of access or a person allowed to access them within the purpose of the submission order.
  (4) Where a party fails to comply with an order for submission of data without good reasons, the court may deem the argument of the other party on the description of the data to be true.
  (5) In cases falling under paragraph (4), where it is significantly difficult for a party who filed a request for the submission of data to make a detailed assertion on the descriptions of the data and it is also difficult to expect that the fact is proved by any other evidence, the court may deem that the argument of the party on the fact he or she intends to prove by the descriptions of the data is true.
  [This Article Added on Feb. 6, 2024]

Article 55-9 (Confidentiality order) (1) If a party establishes a prima facie case of the party's trade secret on any of the following grounds in a lawsuit over a monetary compensation for a service invention, the court may, upon the party's request, order the other party (if the party is a corporation, its representative), the person who represents the other party in the lawsuit, or any other person who becomes aware of the trade secret in the course of the legal proceedings not to use the trade secret for any purpose other than conduct of the lawsuit, and not to disclose the trade secret to a person other than the persons in receipt of the order under this paragraph in connection with the trade secret; provided, this shall not apply where the other party (if the party is a corporation, its representative), the person who represents the other party in the lawsuit, or any other person who becomes aware of the trade secret in the course of the legal proceedings has already acquired the trade secret by any means other than inspection of trial briefs and examination of evidence referred to in subparagraph 1 before the request is filed:
  1. That the trade secret is included in a trial brief already submitted or to be submitted, in a piece of evidence already examined or to be examined, or data submitted or to be submitted pursuant to Article 55-8 (3);
  2. That the trade secret referred to in subparagraph 1 is likely to hinder the party's business operations, if used or disclosed for any purpose other than the conduct of the relevant lawsuit, and thus the use or disclosure of such trade secret needs to be restricted to prevent such hindrance.
  (2) A request for an order under paragraph (1) (hereinafter referred to as "confidentiality order") shall be filed in writing, stating the following:
  1. The person to whom the confidentiality order shall be issued;
  2. Facts sufficient for specifying the trade secret to be protected by the confidentiality order;
  3. Facts relevant to the grounds under the subparagraphs of paragraph (1).
  (3) When a court decides to issue a confidentiality order, it shall serve a written decision on the person to whom the confidentiality order is issued.
  (4) A confidentiality order shall take effect when the written decision under paragraph (3) is served on the person to whom the confidentiality order is issued.
  (5) An immediate appeal may be filed against a decision to reject a request for a confidentiality order, with or without prejudice.
  [This Article Added on Feb. 6, 2024]

Article 55-10 (Revocation of confidentiality order) (1) If a person who has requested or has been issued a confidentiality order fails or ceases to satisfy the requirements under Article 55-9 (1), he or she may apply for the revocation of the confidentiality order to the court that keeps the relevant trial records (where no such court exists, referring to the court that has issued the confidentiality order).
  (2) When a court makes a decision on a request to revoke a confidentiality order, it shall serve a written decision on the applicant for such request and the other party.
  (3) An immediate appeal may be filed against a decision on revocation of a confidentiality order.
  (4) A decision to revoke a confidentiality order shall take effect when it becomes final and conclusive.
  (5) Where a person has been issued a confidentiality order in connection with the relevant trade secret, other than the person who filed a request for revocation of the confidentiality order or the other party, the court that renders a decision to revoke the confidentiality order shall immediately notify the person of such revocation.
  [This Article Added on Feb. 6, 2024]

Article 55-11 (Notification of request for inspection of trial records) (1) If a decision under Article 163 (1) of the Civil Procedure Act is rendered with respect to trial records concerning a lawsuit in which a confidentiality order had been issued (excluding lawsuits in which a confidentiality order has been revoked), and an applicant for a request to inspect confidential records, etc. prescribed in that paragraph is not subject to the confidentiality order in the relevant lawsuit, a Grade IV, V, VI, or VII court official (hereafter referred to as "court official" in this Article) shall notify the applicant for a request under Article 163 (1) of the Civil Procedure Act (excluding the applicant for a request for inspection, etc.; hereafter the same shall also apply in paragraph (3)) of the fact that the request for inspection, etc. was filed immediately after the request.
  (2) In cases falling under paragraph (1), no court official shall permit inspection, etc. of confidential records under paragraph (1) by an applicant for a request until two weeks have passed from the filing date of the request under paragraph (1) (or, if an application for a confidentiality order against the person who has filed the request is made within that period, until the time a decision on such request becomes final and conclusive).
  (3) Paragraph (2) shall not apply where all parties who have filed a request under Article 163 (1) of the Civil Procedure Act agree to allow the person who has filed a request for inspection, etc. under paragraph (1) to inspect the confidential records under paragraph (1).
  [This Article Added on Feb. 6, 2024]

Article 56 (Delegation of authority) (1) The Minister of Intellectual Property may delegate part of his or her authority vested under this Act to the Special Metropolitan City Mayer, a Metropolitan City Mayor, a Special Self-Governing City Mayor, a Do Governor, or the Governor of a Special Self-Governing Province, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) The Minister of Intellectual Property may entrust part of his or her duties under this Act to the association, the Korea Invention Promotion Association, the Protection Agency, the head of an invention agency (referring to the head of an agency to which a public official, etc. belongs as at the time the public official, etc. makes an invention), the Korea Patent Attorneys Association established under Article 9 of the Patent Attorney Act, or an agency designated as a technology trading agency pursuant to Article 10 of the Technology Transfer and Commercialization Promotion Act, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on May 18, 2015; Feb. 4, 2020; Apr. 20, 2021; Feb. 3, 2022; Feb. 6, 2024; Oct. 1, 2025>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Jul. 30, 2013]

Article 57 (Hearings) The Minister of Intellectual Property shall hold a hearing to make any of the following dispositions: <Amended on Jul. 30, 2013; May 18, 2015; Feb. 3, 2022; Jan. 3, 2023; Feb. 6, 2024; Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Revocation of designation, or suspension of business operations, of a specialized institution or organization under Articles 40-3 (4) and 50-2 (4);
  2. Revocation of certification under Article 11-2 (4);
  3. Cancellation of registration of a regional intellectual property center under Article 24 (1) or suspension of its business operations;
  4. Revocation of designation of an evaluation institute under Article 31 (1) or suspension of its business operations;
  5. Deleted. <Feb. 6, 2024>

Article 57-2 (Re-examination of regulation) The Minister of Intellectual Property shall examine the appropriateness of the following matters every three years, counting from each base date specified in the following (referring to the period that ends on the day before the base date of every third year) and shall take measures, such as making improvements: <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Standards for registration of a regional intellectual property center and application procedures under Article 23: January 1, 2015;
  2. Standards for administrative dispositions on a regional intellectual property center under Article 24: January 1, 2015;
  3. Standards for designation of an evaluation institute under Article 28: January 1, 2015;
  4. Standards for administrative dispositions on an evaluation institute under Article 31: January 1, 2015;
  5. Deleted. <Feb. 6, 2024>
  6. Deleted; <Feb. 6, 2024>
  7. Standards for designation of a specialized institution or organization and standards for administrative dispositions on a specialized institution or organization under Article 50-2: January 1, 2015;
  8. Standards for imposition of an administrative fine under Article 60 (1) 4: January 1, 2015.
  [This Article Added on May 18, 2015]

CHAPTER VIII PENALTY PROVISIONS

Article 58 (Penalty provisions) (1) A person who has violated a confidentiality order issued under Article 55-9 (1) without good cause at home or abroad shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than five years or by a fine not exceeding 50 million won. <Added on Feb. 6, 2024>
  (2) A person who discloses details of a service invention with intent to acquire unjust benefits or inflict loss on an employer, etc. in violation of Article 19 shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than three years or by a fine not exceeding 30 million won. <Amended on Feb. 6, 2024>
  (3) A person who violates Article 31-7 shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than one year or by a fine not exceeding 10 million won. <Added on Jan. 3, 2023; Feb. 6, 2024>
  (4) A prosecution for a crime under paragraph (1) or (2) may be instituted only where an employer, etc. files a criminal complaint. <Amended on Jan. 3, 2023; Feb. 6, 2024>

Article 58-2 (Joint penalty provisions) If the representative, an agent, or an employee of, or any other person employed by, a corporation or an organization commits the violation prescribed in Article 58 (3) in conducting the business affairs of the corporation or organization, the corporation or organization shall be punished by a fine prescribed in that Article in addition to punishing the violators accordingly; provided, this shall not apply where the corporation or organization has not been negligent in exercising due care and supervision over the relevant business affairs to prevent such violation. <Amended on Feb. 6, 2024>
  [This Article Added on Jan. 3, 2023]

Article 59 (Legal fiction as public officials for purposes of applying penalty provisions) (1) The mediators of the Committee who are not public officials, and the executive officers and employees of the evaluation management center, the Business Support Center, the Korea Invention Promotion Association, and the Protection Agency shall be deemed to be public officials for purposes of applying the penalty provisions under the Criminal Act and other statutes. <Amended on Feb. 4, 2020; Feb. 3, 2022; Jan. 3, 2023; Feb. 6, 2024>
  (2) The executive officers and employees (limited to the executive officers and employees engaged in duties entrusted) of an institution (excluding the evaluation management center, the Business Support Center, the Korea Invention Promotion Association, and the Protection Agency) entrusted with duties under this Act by the Minister of Intellectual Property shall be deemed to be public officials for the purposes of applying Articles 129 through 132 of the Criminal Act. <Amended on Feb. 4, 2020; Feb. 3, 2022; Jan. 3, 2023; Feb. 6, 2024; Oct. 1, 2025>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 18, 2015]

Article 60 (Administrative fines) (1) Any of the following persons shall be subject to an administrative fine not exceeding 10 million won: <Amended on Jul. 30, 2013; May 18, 2015; Feb. 4, 2020; Feb. 3, 2022>
  1. A person who fails to organize a deliberative committee or fails to undergo deliberation, in violation of Article 18 (3);
  2. A person who discloses to any other person the details of a service invention he or she becomes aware of in the course of performing his or her duties while participating in a deliberative committee as an advisor, in violation of Article 19 (2);
  3. Deleted; <Feb. 6, 2024>
  4. A person who uses the name, a regional intellectual property center, without registration under Article 23 (3), in violation of paragraph (5) of that Article;
  5. A person who uses the name, Korea Invention Promotion Association, in violation of Article 52 (5);
  6. A person who uses the name, Korea Intellectual Property Protection Agency, in violation of Article 55-2 (4);
  7. Deleted. <Feb. 6, 2024>
